WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:01.970
Deadpool.

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:03.350
Everybody loves him!

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:04.870
And with good reason.

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:07.370
As a character, he's irreverent and unpredictable.

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:11.129
He tends to shake things up in both comics
and movies, asking the real questions that

00:00:11.129 --> 00:00:12.669
fans have always wondered about.

00:00:12.669 --> 00:00:13.679
“I am going to wait out here ok.

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:16.460
It’s a big house, it is funny that I only
ever see two of you.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:20.520
It’s almost like the studio couldn’t afford
another Xman.”

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:24.080
Deadpool wasn't always the butt-whomping,
fast-talking, immortal mercenary that he is

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:25.080
today.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:30.140
Here's a brief history of Deadpool's weirdest
moments in comics, movies, and video games.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:31.760
Stroke Of Genius

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:36.090
To be clear up front: Deadpool is pretty much
an unoriginal character.

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:39.800
Writer Fabian Nicieza was trying to cobble
together a coherent script for Marvel's New

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:44.329
Mutants based on artist Rob Liefeld's plots
and characters, when he noticed that Liefeld

00:00:44.329 --> 00:00:47.510
basically copied the DC Comics' character
Deathstroke.

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:51.610
Like Deathstroke, Liefeld's Deadpool was also
a super-agile mercenary, and the two were

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:55.149
visually similar enough to make the weird
copy unmistakable.

00:00:55.149 --> 00:01:00.670
Bringing the ouroboros of unoriginality full-circle,
Nicieza gave Deadpool the real name of Wade

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:04.809
Wilson, in reference to Deathstroke's real
name, Slade Wilson.

00:01:04.809 --> 00:01:06.210
Cable Subscription

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:12.060
Deadpool first appeared in 1991, in New Mutants
#98, where he was written as a mercenary who

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:15.220
came out of nowhere to attack the hero known
as Cable.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:16.390
Don't know who Cable is?

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:20.890
A co-creation of Liefeld and writer Louise
Simonson, Cable is the son of Cyclops and

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:22.390
a clone of Jean Grey.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:23.500
And he's from the future!

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:24.500
“What’s happening?”

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:29.580
Cable made every X-Men comic infinitely more
complicated by introducing time travel and

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.350
alternate timelines.

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:36.080
But despite the fact that the two don't actually
have much in common, Deadpool and Cable have

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:38.900
been linked in the comics ever since.

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:39.900
Merc With A Mouth

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:44.900
Eventually, Deadpool outgrew his traditional
villainous roots and became a fan favorite.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:48.950
That's thanks in large part to what's now
known as his signature schtick: breaking the

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:51.580
fourth wall and addressing readers directly.

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:56.110
The in-story explanation is that Deadpool
is just nuts, so of course he thinks he's

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:57.420
a fictional character.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:01.850
But fans can't help but love a hyper-violent
anti-hero who has just as much to say about

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:04.030
the state of superheroes as they do.

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:07.360
“When it comes to licking wounds, there’s
no better place than home.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.460
And I share that home with someone you’ve
met the old blind lady from the laundrymat,

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:13.460
Al”

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:15.280
“God i miss cocaine”

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.420
“Her, ooh, 4th wall break inside a 4th wall
break.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.310
That’s like 16 walls.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:23.950
Ultimate Deadpool

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:28.370
Mainstream Marvel's Deadpool is the guy that
everyone knows and loves, but Marvel's handling

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:32.790
of the character across different universes
and media has often been kind of...terrible.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:36.980
In an attempt to return Deadpool to his villainous
origins, the Ultimate Marvel line of comics

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:41.930
saw a Deadpool who was an insane cyborg war
veteran with no skin and clear helmet to keep

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:46.430
his brains from falling out, hell-bent on
destroying all mutants.

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:52.030
Not only was Ultimate Deadpool hunting mutants
for a TV show, but he was also a shapeshifter.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:55.230
Because it's surprisingly easy to write stupid
comics.

00:02:55.230 --> 00:03:00.330
Fortunately for all comic book fans, the real
Deadpool eventually stuck a sword right into

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:01.410
that crappy one.

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:03.200
“Man, that felt good.”

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:04.730
Agent X

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:08.420
For a little while in the early 2000s, Marvel
convinced comic readers that they'd killed

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:12.730
off Deadpool and brought him back as a new
character called Agent X.

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:13.870
Why?

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:18.080
Because Deadpool's sales numbers were flagging,
and dropping an X into a Marvel title has

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:19.510
always been a cash machine.

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:24.550
It wasn't enough to save Deadpool, and it
was also just enough to frustrate dedicated

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:27.379
Deadpool fans into dropping the book entirely.

00:03:27.379 --> 00:03:31.470
Even writer Gail Simone was frustrated with
Marvel's editors, and abandoned the book after

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:35.680
less than a year, leaving other writers to
reveal that Agent X wasn't Deadpool after

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:38.489
all, but some weird psychic mash-up of other
characters.

00:03:38.489 --> 00:03:43.269
Fortunately, Deadpool's return to the spotlight
has been pretty successful after that.

00:03:43.269 --> 00:03:44.440
Speechless

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:49.519
It's really hard to forget that Deadpool's
first big-screen appearance was a tragedy.

00:03:49.519 --> 00:03:54.890
In 2009's X-Men Origins: Wolverine, Ryan Reynolds
plays a sexy ninja version of the character,

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:57.940
capable of deflecting machine gun fire with
his swords.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:00.230
But wait—it gets worse.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:04.120
In the course of the film, he dies and is
reassembled into a jambalaya of a guy with

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:06.330
other mutant powers and no mouth.

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:09.720
Yes, the Merc with a Mouth didn't have a mouth.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:12.430
Needless to say, fans weren't pleased.

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:13.819
Deadpool: The Video Game

00:04:13.819 --> 00:04:20.029
X-Men: Origins' Deadpool left such a bad taste
in viewers mouths that gamers flocked to 2013's

00:04:20.029 --> 00:04:24.550
Deadpool video game, which was far truer to
the character's form.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:29.060
“Ha, what a bunch of chumps!”

00:04:29.069 --> 00:04:33.490
Making repeated references to the game's budget
and script, Deadpool breaks the fourth wall

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:37.580
and does enough ridiculous stuff for it to
feel like a legitimate entry into Deadpool

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:38.580
canon.

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:40.930
“Well, isn’t that handy.”

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:44.379
Unfortunately, critics gave the game a lukewarm
reception.

00:04:44.379 --> 00:04:49.479
Even still, for lots of fans, it was one step
closer to a true Deadpool movie...the one

00:04:49.479 --> 00:04:51.820
everyone had been waiting for.

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:53.249
Reynolds' Redemption

00:04:53.249 --> 00:04:57.719
As unhappy as Deadpool's appearance in X-Men:
Origins made the fans, no one was more disappointed

00:04:57.719 --> 00:04:59.270
than Ryan Reynolds himself.

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:03.020
“I remember telling one of the studio execs,
you guys are gonna, they’re gonna, people

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:04.629
are gonna go nuts over this.

00:05:04.629 --> 00:05:05.629
And he was like, I know right.

00:05:05.629 --> 00:05:06.849
And I was like, no not in a good way.”

00:05:06.849 --> 00:05:07.849
“No nuts”

00:05:07.849 --> 00:05:09.229
“No, no, no people went insane.”

00:05:09.229 --> 00:05:11.689
“The deadpool character, you can’t do
this to the deadpool character.

00:05:11.689 --> 00:05:14.289
And they just said were, were gonna, ital
work, watch.”

00:05:14.289 --> 00:05:17.919
In the years that followed, Reynolds became
something of a champion for the long-delayed

00:05:17.919 --> 00:05:20.039
spin-off movie starring the character.

00:05:20.039 --> 00:05:25.159
In 2012, 20th Century Fox commissioned CGI
test-footage from visual effects artist Tim

00:05:25.159 --> 00:05:26.159
Miller.

00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:29.979
The result was a two-minute scene starring
Reynolds, who provided the voice for an over-the-top,

00:05:29.979 --> 00:05:36.110
crazy-violent Deadpool doing what he does
best: cracking jokes and skulls in equal measure.

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:41.550
Still, Fox's executives sat on the footage
for two years...until it was somehow leaked

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:44.710
onto the Internet in 2014.

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:50.180
Fans went wild, the film was greenlit, and
in 2016, Deadpool broke weekend box office

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:57.099
records for an R-rated movie, earning $135
million in just three days.

00:05:57.099 --> 00:05:58.799
Eat that, Deathstroke.

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:02.720
“I’m alive, I’m alive!”

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:11.640
“Oww”

00:06:12.349 --> 00:06:13.349
Thanks for watching!

00:06:13.349 --> 00:06:16.120
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:19.110
And leave us a comment to let us know about
your favorite Deadpool moment...

